{#
  The step that locks the tools `mise.toml` names, and for a published deployment installs them.

  Every deployment that runs the tasks declares tools there -- at least the UI opener, and a published one its binaries
  as `cargo:` tools -- each requested as `latest`, so without a committed lockfile two machines can install different
  releases from the same files. A local deployment compiles what it runs from the working tree, so locking is all it
  needs here. A published one also installs, since `task.run_auto_install` is off and the first task would otherwise
  die on a command it cannot find rather than fetching it.

  `NPM_CONFIG_USERCONFIG` is exported rather than left to the `[env]` beside it, which carries the same value. mise
  computes that entry but does not apply it to its own tool resolution, so the npm client it embeds reads no user
  config, falls back to registry.npmjs.org, and reports the scoped module packages as `package not found` -- they exist
  only on GitHub Packages. Exported into the process, the same file resolves against the right registry, for the lock
  and the install alike. The `[env]` entry stays because the tasks do get it.
#}
{% if published %}
## Install

Lock and fetch the binaries and module packages the `mise.toml` tasks name before the first run.
`GITHUB_TOKEN` has to be set: GitHub Packages rejects an unauthenticated read even for a public
package. The registry configuration is exported rather than relied on from `mise.toml`, because
mise does not apply its own `[env]` to the resolution these commands perform:

```bash
export NPM_CONFIG_USERCONFIG="$PWD/npmrc"
mise lock
mise install --locked
```

`--locked` installs exactly what the lockfile records, and fails rather than resolving anew
when it is missing an entry.
{% else %}
## Lock The Tools

Before the first run, lock the tools `mise.toml` names:

```bash
mise lock
```
{% endif %}

Commit `mise.lock` alongside the deployment, together with the `.mise/locks/` directory when
mise writes one -- it holds the dependency graph of each npm tool, which `mise.lock` refers to.
The two record the version and checksum every `latest` in `mise.toml` resolved to, for each of
mise's common platforms, so every later install -- on any machine -- fetches the same binaries
rather than whatever is newest that day. Move to newer releases deliberately, with
`mise lock --bump`, and commit the result.
